+# Phase 6N-1 AppLoad build and verification
+
+## Baseline
+
+```sh
+git switch --detach v0.5.3
+git switch -c spike/phase-6n1-native-ink
+```
+
+The POC must remain based on commit
+`5bb34a362f09f753f18bd6261558f8e2737aacdb`. Do not merge current AppLoad
+`master` or update Xovi/QTFB behavior wholesale.
+
+## Host-verifiable tests
+
+These tests need only a C++17 compiler and do not access a device:
+
+```sh
+chmod +x tests/run-host-tests.sh
+./tests/run-host-tests.sh
+```
+
+They verify the protocol ABI/bounds, dirty-region/hit-test geometry, and the
+v0.5.3 scaled partial-update correction—including one-pixel rectangles that
+previously collapsed to zero.
+
+## Target AppLoad build
+
+AppLoad requires Linux and the correct reMarkable AArch64 SDK/toolchain. The
+official reMarkable developer documentation states that Qt Quick target builds
+require Linux and the matching device SDK. Do not build with macOS Qt or an
+unrelated firmware SDK.
+
+Using the AppLoad release-build container on an isolated Linux builder:
+
+```sh
+git clone https://github.com/asivery/xovi.git /work/xovi
+export XOVI_REPO=/work/xovi
+
+# Enter eeems/remarkable-toolchain:latest-rmpp using the builder's approved
+# container runner, then inside the container:
+. /opt/codex/*/*/environment-setup-*
+apt-get update
+apt-get install -y qt6-base-dev
+ln -sf /usr/lib/qt6/libexec/rcc /usr/local/bin/rcc
+cd /work/rm-appload/xovi
+./make.sh
+```
+
+Expected output:
+
+```text
+xovi/appload.so
+```
+
+Do not build or replace the QTFB shim for the first POC; its source is
+unchanged. Record the compiler environment, source commit, artifact size, and
+SHA-256. Run `readelf -d` and reject unexpected dependencies before any device
+approval request.
+
+## Current workspace build status
+
+- Host protocol/geometry/scaling harness: buildable and required to pass.
+- AppLoad Qt/Xovi `.so`: requires the missing Linux/reMarkable SDK and was not
+  produced on the macOS study host.
+- Device installation: prohibited until explicit approval.
diff --git a/docs/PHASE6N1_NATIVE_INK_PROTOCOL.md b/docs/PHASE6N1_NATIVE_INK_PROTOCOL.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..46937f9
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/PHASE6N1_NATIVE_INK_PROTOCOL.md
@@ -0,0 +1,137 @@
+# Phase 6N-1 native ink protocol
+
+Status: source proof-of-concept based exactly on AppLoad v0.5.3. The legacy
+QTFB packet ABI is unchanged. Native ink is opt-in through
+`"nativeInk": true` in an external QTFB application's manifest.
+
+## Why this is a side protocol
+
+QTFB v0.5.3's `ServerMessage` is a fixed-size ABI used by existing clients.
+Making it larger would risk truncation and break every client that receives
+`sizeof(ServerMessage)`. The POC therefore keeps `/tmp/qtfb.sock` compatible
+and adds one bounded `SOCK_SEQPACKET` socket per opted-in QTFB framebuffer:
+
+```text
+/tmp/appload-native-ink-<QTFB_KEY>.sock
+```
+
+The socket is created with mode `0600`; the server checks same-UID peer
+credentials where the platform exposes `SO_PEERCRED`. It carries only native
+ink transactions and aggregate metrics. Normal finger gestures continue on
+the legacy QTFB path while Marker proximity is false.
+
+## Raw input preservation
+
+`NativeInkInput` opens `/dev/input/event2` and `/dev/input/event3` with
+`O_RDONLY | O_NONBLOCK | O_CLOEXEC`. It never calls `EVIOCGRAB`, never writes
+to an input node, and never changes an event clock.
+
+Pen frames retain, in memory:
+
+- `BTN_TOOL_PEN` / `BTN_TOOL_RUBBER` identity;
+- `BTN_TOUCH` contact;
+- `ABS_PRESSURE`, `ABS_DISTANCE`, `ABS_TILT_X/Y`;
+- normalized position;
+- original `input_event.time` and a local monotonic receipt timestamp;
+- a monotonic in-process sequence.
+
+Touch frames retain up to ten slots with tracking/contact state, major/minor
+when advertised, pressure, distance, tool type, normalized position, original
+timestamp, receipt timestamp, and sequence. Touch frames stay within the
+native bridge for the POC: QTFB finger events are forwarded only when Marker
+proximity is false and suppressed as a group while proximity is true. No raw
+touch or pen coordinates are written to diagnostics.
+
+## Wire format
+
+The ABI is little-endian, packed, and versioned. `Header` is 48 bytes and
+`Point` is 24 bytes. Host tests assert both sizes.
+
+```text
+Header {
+  magic = "INK1"
+  protocol_major = 1
+  protocol_minor = 0
+  message_type
+  header_bytes = 48
+  payload_bytes <= 32768
+  sequence
+  original_event_timestamp_ns
+  stroke_id
+  flags
+  point_count <= 128
+}
+
+Point {
+  x, y                    # transient screen-space float
+  original_timestamp_ns
+  pressure_u16
+  tilt_x_i16, tilt_y_i16
+  tool                    # PEN or REAR_ERASER
+}
+```
+
+Message types:
+
+| Type | Purpose |
+| --- | --- |
+| `HELLO` | Screen size, input capabilities, hard bounds |
+| `STROKE_BEGIN` | Begin one black-pen transaction |
+| `STROKE_POINTS` | Up to 128 points; KOReader buffers but does not paint live |
+| `STROKE_END` | Commit and persist through existing InkService/InkStore |
+| `ERASE_BEGIN` | Begin one grouped rear-eraser transaction |
+| `ERASE_POINTS` | Batched eraser path; KOReader mutates vectors without per-point repaint |
+| `ERASE_END` | Persist the grouped deletion |
+| `METRICS` | Aggregate counters/timing only; sent after each completed contact |
+| `GOODBYE` | Session teardown |
+
+Hard bounds:
+
+- 32 KiB payload per packet;
+- 128 points per packet;
+- 10,000 points per stroke in both sender and receiver;
+- 1,024 queued native frames before oldest-frame drop and counter increment;
+- 32 messages drained per 10 ms KOReader poll;
+- one same-UID client.
+
+KOReader commits only on `STROKE_END`. Duplicate persistent stroke IDs are
+idempotent. An incomplete, oversized, out-of-order, or unmatched transaction
+is discarded without changing the sidecar.
+
+## Input gating
+
+When `nativeInk` is active:
+
+- all generic AppLoad mouse/pen forwarding is suppressed so raw Marker and
+  rear-eraser events cannot reach KOReader twice;
+- all AppLoad touch forwarding is suppressed while raw Marker/rear proximity
+  is active;
+- normal AppLoad finger forwarding resumes immediately on proximity leave.
+
+This is deliberately conservative. Touch-major/pressure classification can be
+added after clean physical distributions exist, without changing the wire ABI.
+
+## Presentation and persistence split
+
+The native `QQuickPaintedItem` owns live black ink, native current-session
+rear erasure, dirty-rectangle union, and update submission. It never requests
+a QTFB or full/quality repaint.
+
+KOReader polls the side socket and writes only completed transactions using the
+existing `InkStroke`, `InkAnchor`, and `InkStore` schema. It performs no
+per-point UIManager repaint. The native overlay retains current-session strokes
+so persistence cannot stall live presentation.
+
+## Diagnostics
+
+The native side maintains aggregate counts and durations for:
+
+- raw frames received;
+- samples rendered, handed to KOReader, and dropped;
+- display updates submitted/coalesced;
+- QPainter call count and cumulative paint time;
+- raw-event-to-receipt, receipt-to-update-submit, and raw-event-to-submit time
+  when the raw event clock is comparable to `CLOCK_MONOTONIC`.
+
+No coordinate, point payload, text, or raw event sequence appears in the
+diagnostic summary. Optical pen-to-visible latency remains a camera measurement.
